<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ajmer-Merwara</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1818">1818</a>–<a href="/years/1950">1950</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Ajmer-Merwara Province</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ajmer-Merwara was a small province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>, formed from the district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a>, ceded by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Marathas</a> to the British in 1818, and the hill tract of Merwara. Ruled directly by a chief commissioner among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a>, it became the Part C state of Ajmer in 1950 and was merged into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a> under the States Reorganisation Act in 1956.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> territory was ceded to the British by Daulat Rao Sindhia of Gwalior in a treaty dated 25 June 1818, and in May 1823 Udaipur State ceded the Merwara part. The two districts were physically cut off from the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, forming an enclave among the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana">Rajputana</a>. Unlike those states, whose own rulers acknowledged British suzerainty, Ajmer-Merwara was governed directly, first by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-india-company">East India Company</a> through superintendents, the first of them appointed in July 1818.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The territory came under the Bengal Presidency until 1861, when it became part of the North-Western Provinces. The two districts were placed under a single commissioner in 1842 and separated again in 1856. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Rebellion of 1857</a> the Company's powers passed to the Crown, and Ajmer-Merwara was run by a chief commissioner subordinate to the Governor-General's agent for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajputana-agency">Rajputana Agency</a>. On 1 April 1871 it became a separate province under the name Ajmer-Merwara-Kekri. From independence on 15 August 1947 until 1950 it remained a province of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><p>Its last chief commissioner under British rule was Hiranand Rupchand Shivdasani, in office from December 1944 to August 1947. Earlier holders of the post and of the agency included Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lewis-pelly">Lewis Pelly</a>, Alfred Comyns Lyall and Henry Montgomery Lawrence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The province covered 2,710 square miles (7,000 km2). Its centre was the plateau on which the town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ajmer">Ajmer</a> stands, ringed by hills of the Aravalli Range. The ridge between Ajmer and Nasirabad divides the waters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>: rain on its south-eastern slopes drains through the Chambal towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-of-bengal">Bay of Bengal</a>, and rain on the north-western side through the Luni River to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rann-of-kutch">Rann of Kutch</a>. Lying on the edge of the arid zone, between the reach of the south-western and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monsoon">monsoons</a>, Ajmer-Merwara received little rain, and its farmers could never rely on two good harvests in a row.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ajmer-Merwara” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>When British rule ended in August 1947, Rajputana consisted of some two dozen princely states and chiefships, among them ancient Rajput kingdoms such as Mewar (Udaipur), Marwar (Jodhpur), Jaipur and Bikaner, together with the small British province of <b>Ajmer-Merwara</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-state-of-rajasthan">United State of Rajasthan · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
